{
  "projects": [
    {
      "id": "urban_interface",
      "title": "Urban Interface",
      "year": "2025",
      "category": "Interaction Design",
      "role": "UX Research & UI Design",
      "tools": "Figma, Arduino, P5.js",
      "hero": "https://picsum.photos/seed/urbaninterface/1400/700",
      "sections": [
        {
          "type": "section",
          "number": "01",
          "heading": "The Challenge",
          "body": [
            "Public spaces are increasingly mediated by digital systems — yet most of those systems are designed for efficiency rather than experience. Wayfinding is utilitarian. Information is generic. The <strong class=\"text-black font-medium\">emotional and social dimensions of moving through a city</strong> are almost entirely ignored.",
            "This project asked a different question: what if public interfaces could respond to the <strong class=\"text-black font-medium\">rhythms, moods, and needs</strong> of the people around them — rather than simply broadcasting static information?"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "number": "02",
          "heading": "Research",
          "body": [
            "Field research at three urban locations combined observation with structured interviews. Over three weeks, we mapped pedestrian flows, dwell times, and moments of hesitation — building a detailed picture of how people actually navigate shared space versus how designers assume they do."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "images-2col",
          "images": [
            "https://picsum.photos/seed/urbang1/800/600",
            "https://picsum.photos/seed/urbang2/800/600"
          ],
          "caption": "Left: annotated observation maps from the Hauptplatz site visit. Right: interview session with a long-term resident documenting habitual navigation patterns."
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "number": "03",
          "heading": "Design",
          "body": [
            "Three rounds of prototyping explored different interaction models. The first used proximity sensing to adjust ambient sound; the second introduced visual feedback through floor projection; the third combined both with <strong class=\"text-black font-medium\">real-time crowd density data</strong> pulled from transport APIs.",
            "Each prototype was tested in-situ with 8–12 participants using a think-aloud protocol. Iterations were rapid — a one-week cycle from concept sketch to deployed test."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image-wide",
          "src": "https://picsum.photos/seed/urbanwide2/1400/650",
          "caption": "Prototype 3 installed in the Hauptplatz underpass. Ambient light intensity adapts in real time to crowd density, creating a subtle but legible spatial signal."
        },
        {
          "type": "images-3col",
          "images": [
            "https://picsum.photos/seed/urbansq1/600/600",
            "https://picsum.photos/seed/urbansq2/600/600",
            "https://picsum.photos/seed/urbansq3/600/600"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image-highlight",
          "src": "https://picsum.photos/seed/mosaic3/1400/650",
          "caption": "Detail of the floor projection system during the Hauptplatz pilot."
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "number": "04",
          "heading": "Outcome",
          "body": [
            "Post-pilot surveys across 87 respondents showed a measurable increase in <strong class=\"text-black font-medium\">perceived safety and comfort</strong> in the tested locations. Navigation errors dropped by 34%. More significantly, dwell time in the underpass — previously avoided by most pedestrians after 20:00 — increased by over 60%.",
            "The project demonstrated that ambient, behaviour-responsive interfaces can shift how people experience urban space — without requiring explicit attention or interaction. The work has been submitted for inclusion in the 2025 Urban Design Austria programme."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
